00:23:15,810 --> 00:23:19,490
Mauser. Nineteen to five

478
00:23:29,320 --> 00:23:30,950
Point, Mauser.

479
00:23:30,970 --> 00:23:33,200
Twenty to five.

480
00:23:33,220 --> 00:23:36,830
Kletzki trails Mauser two games to nil.

481
00:23:37,250 --> 00:23:40,410
Game and match point, Mauser.

482
00:23:40,430 --> 00:23:42,160
- Let's have a little fun with this one.
- Okay.

483
00:23:42,170 --> 00:23:43,710
- Have a little fun, all right?
- Gotcha.

484
00:23:44,290 --> 00:23:46,190
Service switches to Kletzki.

485
00:24:16,790 --> 00:24:18,460
Point, Kletzki.

486
00:24:24,010 --> 00:24:26,360
Very well, gentlemen.
Thank you for that. Thank you.

487
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:33,140
Kletzki serves 6-20.

488
00:24:33,160 --> 00:24:36,850
Mauser leads two games to zero.

489
00:24:36,860 --> 00:24:38,690
Match point again for Mr. Mauser.

490
00:24:38,710 --> 00:24:40,670
Your service again, Kletzki.

491
00:24:45,070 --> 00:24:46,720
Point, Mauser!

492
00:24:48,390 --> 00:24:52,680
Mauser wins the game and
match by 21-6. Three games to nil.

493
00:24:52,690 --> 00:24:57,640
Mauser's on to the finals where
he will face Koto Endo of Japan.

494
00:25:00,560 --> 00:25:03,740
{\an8}(Menu)

495
00:25:01,670 --> 00:25:03,790
Get whatever you want. Don't
even look at the prices, okay?

496
00:25:04,010 --> 00:25:04,880
What are you getting?

497
00:25:04,900 --> 00:25:07,470
I'm gonna get the Beef Wellington
and the caviar tasting plate

498
00:25:07,490 --> 00:25:09,340
because they're the most
expensive items on the menu.

499
00:25:09,360 --> 00:25:12,340
Listen, I would like to keep going
back a little bit about the Globetrotters.

500
00:25:12,350 --> 00:25:13,930
- About the Harlem Globetrotters, again?
- Yes.

501
00:25:13,950 --> 00:25:15,470
- I told you, I'm not interested.
- Why not?

502
00:25:15,490 --> 00:25:16,680
'Cause I don't wanna do it.

503
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:18,390
It's great money.

504
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:20,790
And we will travel all over the
world. Have you seen Venice?

505
00:25:20,810 --> 00:25:22,810
- I don't wanna be doing trick
shots for a halftime show - Sydney?

506
00:25:22,830 --> 00:25:24,440
- while people are going to the bathroom.
- Barcelona?

507
00:25:24,460 --> 00:25:25,530
That's beneath me, okay?

508
00:25:25,550 --> 00:25:28,570
- They're treated like royalty.
- Where's the waiter?

509
00:25:28,590 --> 00:25:31,100
- Listen, I know people who have done this.
- Excuse me.

510
00:25:31,110 --> 00:25:32,390
Excuse me.

511
00:25:32,410 --> 00:25:35,600
It's a great opportunity.
We should not miss it.

512
00:25:38,270 --> 00:25:39,520
Marty.

513
00:25:41,680 --> 00:25:43,180
Yes, sir. May I help you, sir?

514
00:25:43,190 --> 00:25:44,260
Hi. Can we order?

515
00:25:44,270 --> 00:25:45,710
Of course, sir. Let
me fetch your waiter.

516
00:25:45,730 --> 00:25:47,030
- Hang on.
- Yes.

517
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:49,330
Uh, I'll be taking care of
the Rockwell tab tonight.

518
00:25:49,340 --> 00:25:52,140
Put it on my room. The
Royal Suite. Marty Mauser.

519
00:25:52,160 --> 00:25:54,400
- And make sure they know it's me, too.
- I will. Leave it to me, sir.

520
00:25:54,410 --> 00:25:55,930
- Don't take no for an answer.
- I won't.

521
00:25:55,950 --> 00:25:58,930
- Marty Mauser. Royal Suite. Go tell 'em.
- Will do, sir.

522
00:26:01,270 --> 00:26:03,390
Okay. Look at me. Don't
look there. Look at me.

523
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:04,890
- Who's Rockwell?
- Some bozo.

524
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:07,270
Look over now. Be subtle
about it. What's he doing?

525
00:26:08,150 --> 00:26:10,020
He hasn't been here before, sir.

526
00:26:10,030 --> 00:26:11,020
He's looking at us.

527
00:26:11,040 --> 00:26:12,100
- Really?
- Yeah.

528
00:26:12,120 --> 00:26:13,610
Okay, keep talking to me. Be normal.

529
00:26:13,620 --> 00:26:15,650
Okay, fine. I want you to reconsider.

530
00:26:15,660 --> 00:26:17,440
We would be a great team.
I wanna do this with you.

531
00:26:17,450 --> 00:26:19,360
Why are you so fixed on
the Harlem Globetrotters?

532
00:26:19,370 --> 00:26:21,410
It's very sad what they do, okay?

533
00:26:21,430 --> 00:26:24,600
Some of the premier athletes on the
planet reduced to acting like circus clowns.

534
00:26:24,610 --> 00:26:25,980
What's he doing now?

535
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:27,340
- He's coming towards us.
- He's coming?

536
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:30,300
- Just be normal. Act casual.
- Okay, fine. Okay.

537
00:26:34,390 --> 00:26:36,580
Excuse me. Do I know you?

538
00:26:37,410 --> 00:26:38,600
I don't think so.

539
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:40,890
And you wanna buy dinner
for my entire table tonight?

540
00:26:40,910 --> 00:26:43,140
- Yeah. Is it okay?
- Why?

541
00:26:43,390 --> 00:26:44,810
What do you mean,
why? Do I need a reason?

542
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:47,230
Let me rephrase that. What do you want?

543
00:26:47,240 --> 00:26:50,180
No, I don't want anything. It's just
my small way of saying thank you.

544
00:26:50,190 --> 00:26:51,950
- Thank you?
- Yeah.

545
00:26:51,970 --> 00:26:53,380
Thank me for what, specifically?

546
00:26:53,390 --> 00:26:54,960
For all your products.

547
00:26:54,970 --> 00:26:57,720
I mean, where would we be without pens?

548
00:26:59,450 --> 00:27:01,860
You know, I have many talents,
but the one I'm most proud of

549
00:27:01,870 --> 00:27:04,840
- is the ability to smell bullshit from a mile away.
- No,

550
00:27:04,850 --> 00:27:07,150
I'm being completely sincere.

551
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:12,220
- Where are you from?
- New York. What about you?

552
00:27:12,230 --> 00:27:14,010
New York too. What do you do?

553
00:27:14,020 --> 00:27:15,500
I'm a professional athlete.

554
00:27:15,510 --> 00:27:17,600
- Really? What sport?
- Table tennis.

555
00:27:17,620 --> 00:27:19,060
Table tennis? That's a sport?

556
00:27:19,070 --> 00:27:20,980
Of course it is. I'm here
competing in the British Open.

557
00:27:20,990 --> 00:27:22,300
I'm playing at Wembley tomorrow

558
00:27:22,310 --> 00:27:24,190
in front of a sold-out crowd
in the finals against Japan.

559
00:27:24,200 --> 00:27:25,480
- I'm here representing the United States.
- Wait, wait, wait.

560
00:27:25,500 --> 00:27:28,090
Japan? Japan has a team here?

561
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:30,190
- That's what I just said.
- How'd they get around the travel ban?

562
00:27:30,210 --> 00:27:32,500
Because table tennis is the
fastest growing sport in Asia.

563
00:27:32,510 --> 00:27:34,180
It's huge over there.

564
00:27:34,190 --> 00:27:35,200
Oh, you're gonna like this.

565
00:27:35,220 --> 00:27:37,620
Do you know what they call their
grip? The way they hold their rackets?

566
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:39,580
- No.
- They call it penholder.

567
00:27:40,910 --> 00:27:41,700
So what?

568
00:27:41,720 --> 00:27:43,070
You can use that.

569
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:44,040
Use it for what?

570
00:27:44,050 --> 00:27:47,280
For an advertisement or something.
I don't know, you're the business guy.

571
00:27:47,290 --> 00:27:49,490
I'm being rude. This is
my associate, Béla Kletzki.

572
00:27:49,500 --> 00:27:50,670
Nice to meet you, sir.

573
00:27:50,690 --> 00:27:52,880
I assume you're a
table tennis player also?

574
00:27:52,900 --> 00:27:56,520
I am. I am. Actually, I was the
World Champion from '35 to '39.

575
00:27:56,530 --> 00:27:58,170
He was the World
Champion. I beat him today.

576
00:27:58,190 --> 00:27:59,890
He beat me. But he's
much younger than me.

577
00:27:59,910 --> 00:28:01,630
I'm much better than
him, much more talented.

578
00:28:01,640 --> 00:28:05,860
I can't help but notice this tattoo. You
were in one of the camps, weren't you?

579
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:08,400
Auschwitz-Birkenau.
Yeah. Why do you ask?

580
00:28:08,410 --> 00:28:10,920
My son lost his life liberating you.

581
00:28:13,070 --> 00:28:14,990
I'm sorry for your loss.

582
00:28:15,400 --> 00:28:17,230
I thought you were
liberated by the Soviets.

583
00:28:17,240 --> 00:28:18,790
- We were. It's Soviets.
- It wasn't the Americans.

584
00:28:18,810 --> 00:28:21,680
I didn't mean it literally. He
was serving in the South Pacific.

585
00:28:21,700 --> 00:28:23,920
Well, what happened to him?

586
00:28:23,930 --> 00:28:25,870
What do you mean? He was killed.

587
00:28:26,790 --> 00:28:27,940
You find that funny?

588
00:28:27,960 --> 00:28:31,910
Oh, God. No, I just get
nervous laughter sometimes.

589
00:28:31,920 --> 00:28:33,130
If it's any consolation,

590
00:28:33,150 --> 00:28:35,720
I'll be dropping a third atom
bomb on their heads tomorrow.

591
00:28:35,740 --> 00:28:37,750
Yeah, I'm sure his mother's
gonna get a lot of comfort

592
00:28:37,760 --> 00:28:39,620
that you're playing
ping pong in his honor.

593
00:28:39,640 --> 00:28:41,800
It's not that big of a
deal, but I appreciate it.

594
00:28:41,810 --> 00:28:44,430
Tell him-- He used to
diffuse bombs for the Nazis.

595
00:28:44,450 --> 00:28:46,180
Tell him the story you told me about--

596
00:28:46,190 --> 00:28:47,910
- I've got a whole table of people waiting.
- No, no, no, wait, wait.

597
00:28:47,930 --> 00:28:49,770
You're gonna love this story. Tell him.

598
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:52,650
- You wanna hear it?
- Not really, but go ahead.

599
00:28:52,670 --> 00:28:54,110
No, tell him. It's beautiful.

600
00:28:54,120 --> 00:28:56,010
Okay, I'll make it quick. So...

601
00:28:56,020 --> 00:28:59,710
when I first got to the
camp, I was put into housing.

602
00:28:59,720 --> 00:29:02,620
Lucky for me, there was an
officer who recognized me.

603
00:29:02,640 --> 00:29:07,320
He had seen me in 1935 at the
World Championships in Prague.

604
00:29:07,330 --> 00:29:10,910
This guy was a real fan.
I mean, a real fan, okay?

605
00:29:10,920 --> 00:29:13,810
You're telling me he let you live
because you played ping pong?

606
00:29:13,820 --> 00:29:15,870
They respected my skills, yeah.

607
00:29:17,370 --> 00:29:21,790
So then, they taught me
how to dismantle SC bombs.

608
00:29:22,140 --> 00:29:23,220
Okay?

609
00:29:23,240 --> 00:29:26,810
Each morning, they would send
me into the woods with the bombs

610
00:29:26,830 --> 00:29:30,460
on the outskirts of the camp where
I could only do damage to myself.

611
00:29:33,610 --> 00:29:35,190
So one time,

612
00:29:35,880 --> 00:29:37,570
I followed a honeybee.

613
00:29:37,590 --> 00:29:38,730
A bee?

614
00:29:38,750 --> 00:29:40,350
A honeybee,

615
00:29:40,370 --> 00:29:42,390
all the way to its hive.

616
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:46,900
And I smoked all the bees out.

617
00:29:47,990 --> 00:29:50,510
And then broke it open,

618
00:29:50,520 --> 00:29:54,690
and I smeared the honey all over
my chest, my arms, everywhere.

619
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:56,090
Why?

620
00:29:56,110 --> 00:29:57,890
'Cause later that night,

621
00:29:57,900 --> 00:30:02,560
I let all my bunk mates to lick the
honey off of me for nourishment.

622
00:31:44,400 --> 00:31:45,440
Heads.

623
00:31:47,640 --> 00:31:49,220
- Fuck.
- Endo, choice?

624
00:31:49,230 --> 00:31:50,690
[Japanese] Serve.

625
00:31:50,710 --> 00:31:52,210
I'll go to that side.

626
00:31:58,880 --> 00:32:00,960
- On my right...
- Come on, Marty! Come on!

627
00:32:00,980 --> 00:32:04,740
...Marty Mauser of the
United States, ranked second,

628
00:32:04,760 --> 00:32:09,310
versus Koto Endo of Japan, unranked.

629
00:32:09,330 --> 00:32:12,390
The gentlemen will
battle to the best of five

630
00:32:12,400 --> 00:32:14,710
in games to 21 points.

631
00:32:15,410 --> 00:32:18,720
Each game shall be
won by at least two points.

632
00:32:19,820 --> 00:32:21,030
Good luck, gentlemen.

633
00:32:22,050 --> 00:32:23,470
Quiet, please.

634
00:32:24,890 --> 00:32:26,430
All right!

635
00:32:33,970 --> 00:32:37,960
Point, Endo. One-nil.

636
00:32:42,540 --> 00:32:44,420
Come on, America!

637
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:51,490
Point, Endo. Two-nil.

638
00:32:53,770 --> 00:32:55,100
All right, Marty!

639
00:32:56,730 --> 00:32:57,940
Come on, Marty!

640
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:03,640
Point, Endo. Three-nil.

641
00:33:13,270 --> 00:33:17,190
Point, Endo. Four-nil.

642
00:33:22,520 --> 00:33:24,100
Come on, Marty!

643
00:33:30,090 --> 00:33:33,930
Point, Endo. Five-nil.

644
00:33:36,060 --> 00:33:38,500
Service switches to Mauser.

645
00:33:43,520 --> 00:33:46,110
Point, Mauser.

646
00:33:46,120 --> 00:33:48,790
Nine serving 19.

647
00:34:05,210 --> 00:34:09,470
Point, Mauser. 10-19.

648
00:34:09,490 --> 00:34:10,570
Okay, Marty!

649
00:34:34,990 --> 00:34:38,950
Point, Endo. 20-10.

650
00:34:40,160 --> 00:34:41,870
Game point.

651
00:34:45,950 --> 00:34:47,460
Go! Serve.

652
00:35:07,860 --> 00:35:11,860
Point, Endo. 21-10.

653
00:35:11,880 --> 00:35:13,820
Game, Endo.

654
00:35:14,560 --> 00:35:19,020
Endo leads two games to zero.

655
00:35:21,510 --> 00:35:23,830
Point, Mauser.

656
00:35:23,850 --> 00:35:26,180
Eighteen-16.

657
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:44,270
Point, Mauser. 17-18.

658
00:35:44,290 --> 00:35:45,570
Go, Marty! Come on!

659
00:35:45,580 --> 00:35:48,250
Service switches to Mauser.

660
00:36:01,700 --> 00:36:03,540
Fuck.

661
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:11,170
Point, Endo. 17-19.

662
00:36:26,860 --> 00:36:28,010
Oh, fuck.

663
00:36:30,900 --> 00:36:35,640
Point, Endo. 17-20.

664
00:36:35,660 --> 00:36:37,430
And match point.

665
00:36:44,070 --> 00:36:46,030
Quiet, please.

666
00:36:46,620 --> 00:36:49,130
Quiet. Settle down.

667
00:36:50,130 --> 00:36:51,870
Your service, Mauser.

668
00:36:51,880 --> 00:36:54,020
Come on, Marty!

669
00:37:02,010 --> 00:37:03,270
Fuck.

670
00:37:04,730 --> 00:37:06,640
No, bullshit! No!

671
00:37:06,660 --> 00:37:09,310
I want a rematch right now
with a standard-league hard-bat!

672
00:37:09,330 --> 00:37:10,510
That is unacceptable!

673
00:37:10,520 --> 00:37:12,240
What if I had a mechanical arm?

674
00:37:12,250 --> 00:37:14,250
What if I put glue on the
ball? Would that be kosher?

675
00:37:14,270 --> 00:37:17,440
Why, he was getting coached the
entire time! That is unacceptable!

676
00:37:19,450 --> 00:37:22,800
I play real table tennis!
Real table tennis!

677
00:37:23,560 --> 00:37:24,650
Unbelievable.

678
00:37:24,990 --> 00:37:26,070
Unbelievable.

679
00:37:26,080 --> 00:37:28,410
...British Open champion from Japan...

680
00:37:28,420 --> 00:37:29,500
Fuck you!

681
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:31,860
...Koto Endo!

682
00:37:34,210 --> 00:37:36,670
{\an8}(Japanese News)

683
00:37:38,290 --> 00:37:42,640
{\an8}(Special Report)

684
00:37:38,290 --> 00:37:42,180
[Japanese] At the 19th British Table Tennis Open,
Japan has won the championship for the first time.

685
00:37:43,550 --> 00:37:45,880
[Japanese] This is the first time
since the end of the Pacific War

686
00:37:45,900 --> 00:37:49,450
[Japanese] that a Japanese player has
claimed victory on the international stage.

687
00:37:52,140 --> 00:37:54,060
[Japanese] The winner is Koto Endo.

688
00:37:55,820 --> 00:37:58,290
[Japanese] This unknown
Japanese challenger, in the final,

689
00:37:58,310 --> 00:38:02,500
[Japanese] defeated the favored
American contender in a dominant 3–0 victory.

690
00:38:08,650 --> 00:38:10,870
[Japanese] A hundred thousand
people in Tokyo poured into the streets,

691
00:38:10,880 --> 00:38:12,850
[Japanese] like a celebratory parade,

692
00:38:12,860 --> 00:38:14,640
[Japanese] enthusiastically
celebrating his triumph.

693
00:38:16,310 --> 00:38:20,070
[Japanese] Endo, however, remained humble,
saying he would return home as an ordinary craftsman

694
00:38:20,090 --> 00:38:22,920
[Japanese] rather than be
hailed as some so‑called hero.

695
00:38:23,020 --> 00:38:27,730
[Japanese] Endo lost his hearing
during the Tokyo firebombing at age 16.

696
00:38:27,740 --> 00:38:31,380
[Japanese] He honed his senses in silence
and ultimately conquered the sport with iron will.

697
00:38:32,800 --> 00:38:36,400
[Japanese] Now the world's table
tennis spotlight is on our nation.

698
00:38:34,680 --> 00:38:39,890
{\an8}(23rd World Table Tennis Championships)

699
00:38:36,420 --> 00:38:40,100
[Japanese] The host city for this year's
world championships has been set as Tokyo.

700
00:38:40,110 --> 00:38:44,070
[Japanese] American players will surely make
a comeback, determined to avenge their defeat.

701
00:38:44,700 --> 00:38:47,060
[Japanese] But Endo
is not fighting alone.

702
00:38:47,070 --> 00:38:49,430
[Japanese] He is backed by compatriots
united with him in pursuing the nation's hopes,

703
00:38:49,440 --> 00:38:52,840
[Japanese] eight hundred and forty million
fellow citizens sharing an indomitable spirit.

704
00:38:52,980 --> 00:38:55,750
{\an8}(END)

705
00:39:13,770 --> 00:39:15,630
[French] Hello sir, can I help you?

706
00:39:15,640 --> 00:39:17,400
Do you have a Milton
Rockwell reservation?

707
00:39:17,420 --> 00:39:20,690
- [French] Excuse me?
- Milton Rockwell reservation, please.

708
00:39:20,710 --> 00:39:21,850
[French] Ah, Rockwell?

709
00:39:21,860 --> 00:39:24,130
So, listen, Marty, I wanna
personally thank you

710
00:39:24,140 --> 00:39:26,640
for introducing me to table tennis.

711
00:39:27,310 --> 00:39:29,940
I had no idea what a
phenomenon it was in the Asias.

712
00:39:29,950 --> 00:39:31,850
- Oh, yeah. I know.
- And how big it is in Japan!

713
00:39:31,860 --> 00:39:33,580
That's what I was trying
to tell you. It's huge.

714
00:39:33,600 --> 00:39:36,280
So, I'm thinking of incorporating it
into some of the promotional events

715
00:39:36,290 --> 00:39:38,380
we're gonna be doing
over there in the fall.

716
00:39:38,400 --> 00:39:39,750
How so? What are you thinking?

717
00:39:39,770 --> 00:39:42,510
What I wanna do is
stage an exhibition match

718
00:39:42,530 --> 00:39:46,320
around the time of the World
Championships between you and Endo.

719
00:39:46,330 --> 00:39:48,260
- Mmm.
- I think you'd like that idea.

720
00:39:48,280 --> 00:39:49,810
